\begin_layout Subsection

\series bold
Classical Particle Swarm Optimization (PSO)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The classical Particle Swarm Optimization (PSO) algorithm,
 originally introduced by Kennedy and Eberhart 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Kennedy1995"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 is a population-based stochastic optimization technique inspired by the social behavior of bird flocking or fish schooling.
 The algorithm explores an 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

-dimensional continuous search space 
\begin_inset Formula $\ensuremath{\mathcal{S}\subset\mathbb{R}^{n}}$
\end_inset

 using a population of 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

 interacting agents called particles.
 Each particle 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 (
\begin_inset Formula $i\in\{1,2,\ldots,m\})$
\end_inset

 is characterized by its dynamic state parameters,
 consisting of an 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

-dimensional position vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\ensuremath{\mathbf{x}_{i}}=\left[x_{i1},x_{i2},\ldots,x_{in}\right]^{T}$
\end_inset

and a corresponding velocity vector
\begin_inset Formula $\ensuremath{\ensuremath{\ensuremath{\mathbf{v}_{i}}=\left[v_{i1},v_{i2},\dots,v_{in}\right]^{T}.}}$
\end_inset

During the optimization process,
 each particle maintains a memory of its own historical personal best position discovered so far,
 denoted as 
\begin_inset Formula $\ensuremath{\mathbf{p}_{i}}=\left[p_{i1},p_{i2},\dots,p_{in}\right]^{T}$
\end_inset

.
 Concurrently,
 the swarm tracks the absolute global best position achieved by any particle in the entire population,
 defined as
\begin_inset Formula $\ensuremath{\mathbf{p}_{\text{gbest}}=\arg\min_{i\in\{1,\dots,m\}}f\left(\mathbf{p}_{i}\right)}$
\end_inset

,
 where
\begin_inset Formula $f(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 represents the objective function under minimization.
 To balance global exploration and localized exploitation,
 a trade-off extensively evaluated in recent metaheuristic literature 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Kaveh2022,Charilogis2024"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 Shi and Eberhart 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Shi1998"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 introduced the inertia weight factor 
\begin_inset Formula $\ensuremath{\omega}$
\end_inset

.
 At each discrete evolutionary epoch 
\begin_inset Formula $\text{\ensuremath{t}}$
\end_inset

,
 the dynamic trajectories of the particles are updated according to the following stochastic kinematic equations:
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{v}_{i}(t+1)=\omega(t)\cdot\mathbf{v}_{i}(t)+c_{1}\cdot\mathbf{r}_{1}\cdot\left(\mathbf{p}_{i}(t)-\mathbf{x}_{i}(t)\right)+c_{2}\cdot\mathbf{r}_{2}\cdot\left(\mathbf{p}_{\text{gbest}}(t)-\mathbf{x}_{i}(t)\right)
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{x}_{i}(t+1)=\mathbf{x}_{i}(t)+\mathbf{v}_{i}(t+1)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\ensuremath{\omega(t)}$
\end_inset

 is the inertia weight,
 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{2}$
\end_inset

 denote the cognitive and social acceleration coefficients,
 respectively,
 and
\begin_inset Formula $\ensuremath{\mathbf{r}_{1},\mathbf{r}_{2}\sim\mathcal{U}(0,1)}$
\end_inset

 represent independent uniformly distributed random vectors injected to maintain stochastic diversity.
 Despite its mathematical simplicity and high convergence speed,
 the classical formulation is prone to stagnation in complex multimodal landscapes due to rapid loss of population diversity,
 a challenge frequently highlighted in modern optimization benchmarks 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Rebello2021,Gad2022"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
