\begin_layout Subsection

\series bold
Parallel Optimized Particle Swarm Optimization Algorithm
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The distributed framework of the Optimized Particle Swarm Optimization (OPSO) algorithm is engineered by partitioning the global agent population into 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

 distinct subpopulations and it is presented graphically in Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Parallel-OPSO-framework"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

.
 Each subpopulation operates as an autonomous evolutionary unit allocated to an individual processing element,
 enabling simultaneous execution across a shared-memory environment 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "OpenMP2024"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Float figure
placement H
alignment center
wide false
sideways false
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename parallel_pso_islands.png
	lyxscale 45
	width 85col%

\end_inset


\begin_inset Caption Standard

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\size footnotesize
Parallel OPSO framework illustrating thread distribution and flexible inter-island migration topologies.
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "fig:Parallel-OPSO-framework"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
 Before initiating the optimization cycle,
 the multi-swarm configurations are established,
 and the global termination strategy 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{(TS)}$
\end_inset

 is selected.
 To govern decentralized convergence behavior and manage interaction dynamics between the concurrently evolving swarms,
 the architecture introduces a set of control features:
 a topological propagation method 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathrm{NM)}$
\end_inset

,
 a generational communication rate 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathrm{NR)}$
\end_inset

,
 a volumetric quantity of migrated solutions 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathrm{NP)}$
\end_inset

,
 and a localized termination criterion 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathrm{TC)}$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Charilogis2023,Kyrou2026"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The communication frequency across the multi-swarm layout is controlled by the propagation rate 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathrm{NR)}$
\end_inset

,
 which establishes the exact number of generational steps between consecutive data distributions.
 Low values for 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{NR}$
\end_inset

 enforce tight coupling and frequent interaction,
 a configuration that can accelerate convergence velocities but risks collapsing population diversity 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Kyrou2026,daSilveira2020"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Conversely,
 high values for 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{NR}$
\end_inset

 grant the individual islands extended periods of decoupled execution,
 fostering independent exploration of unmapped solution regions.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

In a similar manner,
 the volume of shared elite information is dictated by the parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{NP}$
\end_inset

,
 which defines the number of top-performing vectors transmitted during a migration step.
 Expanding 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{NP}$
\end_inset

 strengthens the collective exploitation capability by injecting high-quality geometric coordinates into target swarms;
 however,
 excessive data injection can induce premature convergence by steering all independent groups toward identical local optima 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Kyrou2026,daSilveira2020,Abadlia2017"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Within this framework,
 the specific bounds for 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{NR}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{NP}$
\end_inset

 are derived through systematic empirical tuning to maintain a healthy balance between localized search refinement and global space exploration,
 with the precise values detailed in the Experimental Results section.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

During every generational step,
 the system monitors each subpopulation to determine if it has met its localized termination criterion 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{(TC)}$
\end_inset

,
 which is evaluated independently via an advanced convergence heuristic known as the Similarity check
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Tsoulos2021"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 The total number of stagnated units is then cross-referenced with a macro-level decision threshold,
 denoted as 
\begin_inset Formula $Q$
\end_inset

.
 This threshold mathematically operationalizes the chosen system-wide termination strategy 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{(TS)}$
\end_inset

 to govern the macro-level optimization loop
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Kyrou2026,Tsoulos2021"
literal "false"

\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
Q=\begin{cases}
1, & \text{if }TS=ANY\\
\lfloor K/2\rfloor+1, & \text{if }TS=MAJORITY\\
K, & \text{if }TS=ALL
\end{cases}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Following the calculation of the threshold metrics,
 the subpopulations proceed with their parallel evolutionary operations,
 executing localized kinematics updates and simulated annealing leader adjustments.
 At generational intervals matching the propagation rate 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathrm{NR)}$
\end_inset

,
 the system triggers the designated migration topology 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathrm{NM})$
\end_inset

 to distribute 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{NP}$
\end_inset

 elite candidate vectors across the multi-threaded network
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Charilogis2023,Kyrou2026,Abadlia2017"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

The iteration counter increments,
 and the status of the threshold criterion 
\begin_inset Formula $Q$
\end_inset

 is evaluated.
 If the conditions for global termination are not fulfilled,
 the concurrent optimization sequence continues.
 Upon satisfying the macro-level stopping condition,
 the framework invokes a memetic refinement sequence,
 executing a rigorous local search directly around the global best solution
\begin_inset Formula $\ensuremath{g_{\text{best}}}$
\end_inset

to secure high-precision mathematical accuracy before returning the final optimized result 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Petalas2007"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

From an architectural standpoint,
 mapping each subpopulation to an isolated thread or processing core facilitates true concurrent execution.
 Synchronization barriers are strictly confined to the scheduled propagation phases,
 allowing the swarms to evolve independently during intermediate generations and significantly minimizing memory access latencies 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Charilogis2023"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Resource utilization is optimized through the selection of the global termination strategies (ANY,
 MAJORITY,
 ALL),
 which prevent thread starvation and idle processing states by eliminating the requirement for all processing elements to terminate at the same time.
 The ANY strategy,
 in particular,
 permits rapid continuation by halting the global loop the moment early convergence is detected on any single thread via the Similarity metric 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Tsoulos2021"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Furthermore,
 the computational footprint of the migration phase remains small,
 as it restricts communication to a limited set of elite solution coordinates.
 This brief coordination phase introduces negligible latency relative to the overall objective function evaluation costs,
 and it significantly enhances optimization performance by enabling effective information sharing across the parallel architecture 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Charilogis2023,Kyrou2026"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection

\series bold
Inter-Swarm Communication Protocol and Migration Topologie
\series default
\shape italic
s
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Within the cooperative execution phase of the parallel framework,
 the communication layer facilitates the distribution of high-quality parameter configurations among the concurrent islands.
 When the scheduled migration interval conditions are satisfied,
 the elite solution vectors identified by a specific subpopulation are transmitted across the shared-memory space to overwrite the lowest-performing coordinates of the designated target swarms.
 The foundational objective of this information routing scheme is to optimize cross-island exploration,
 ensuring that promising topological discoveries are rapidly assimilated into the broader optimization landscape to enhance overall convergence efficiency 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Charilogis2023,Kyrou2026"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

Unlike conventional distributed metaheuristics that rely on static and rigid structural layouts,
 such as ring,
 star,
 or fully-mesh migration topologies,
 the proposed framework introduces a localized,
 dynamically configurable migration model tailored for high-dimensional execution spaces.
 Conventional geometric topologies impose strict synchronization locks and significant thread contention penalties as the execution scale grows,
 which frequently induces severe memory access latencies within shared-memory architectures 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Charilogis2023,Kyrou2026"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 To avoid structural complexity and eliminate excessive communication overhead,
 the parallel architecture relies on a versatile routing protocol operating across four discrete configuration states:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
One-to-One (
\begin_inset Formula $1\text{to}1$
\end_inset

):
 A single source subpopulation,
 selected uniformly at random,
 migrates its historical elite parameter states exclusively to a single target subpopulation,
 which is also designated through a random uniform distribution.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
One-to-All (
\begin_inset Formula $1\text{to}N$
\end_inset

):
 A randomly isolated subpopulation serves as a central broadcaster,
 transmitting its optimal geometric updates to refresh the search vectors of all other 
\begin_inset Formula $K-1$
\end_inset

 active swarms simultaneously.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
All-to-One (
\begin_inset Formula $N\text{to}1$
\end_inset

):
 Every active computational swarm routes its current top-performing vectors toward a single,
 randomly targeted subpopulation,
 consolidating global directional data into a localized spatial window to catalyze structural exploitation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
All-to-All (
\begin_inset Formula $N\text{to}N$
\end_inset

):
 A dense execution mode where every individual subpopulation synchronizes and exchanges its finest candidate solutions with the historical memory matrix of every other swarm in the network.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

By adjusting the combination of these topological routing strategies 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathrm{NM)}$
\end_inset

 alongside the volumetric payload parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathrm{NP)}$
\end_inset

,
 the OPSO framework maintains a fluid balance between regional exploitation and global population diversity 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Charilogis2023,Kyrou2026"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 This dynamic coordination mitigates structural stagnation risks while preserving the low coordination footprint required for efficient execution on multi-core compute clusters.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection

\series bold
Adaptive Inertia weight mechanism
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To achieve a self-regulating balance between global exploration and localized exploitation,
 each autonomous subpopulation discards static or strictly time-dependent parameter scaling.
 Instead,
 an Adaptive Inertia Weight Mechanism is implemented 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Rathore2017"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 adapting the phenotypic stagnation-tracking framework introduced in the Ideal Particle Swarm Optimization (IPSO) architecture 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Charilogis2022"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 This framework evaluates the state of the swarm by monitoring the phenotypic convergence rate across consecutive generations.
 When the global fitness gradient flattens,
 indicating that the multi-swarm trajectory is confined within a local attractor,
 the inertia weight parameter is dynamically adjusted to modulate particle momentum.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

Operationally,
 the system calculates the absolute differential progress of the collective fitness landscape at every iteration.
 If the fitness deviation between sequential states falls below a predefined threshold,
 a stagnation counter increments.
 By calculating the ratio of cumulative stagnant states against the elapsed generational counter,
 a dynamic constraint coefficient is derived.
 Consequently,
 when the subpopulation actively discovers novel structural minima,
 the velocity profile maintains a high exploratory momentum.
 Conversely,
 when severe multi-island stagnation is detected,
 the inertia scales down to its minimum operating boundary,
 dampening particle velocities to focus computational resources strictly on high-precision local exploitation
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Rathore2017,Charilogis2022"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection

\series bold
\shape italic
Mathematical Formulation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The evolutionary state monitoring is governed by evaluating the global fitness volume change at each generation 
\begin_inset Formula $\text{\ensuremath{t}}$
\end_inset

,
 tracking structural stagnation vectors as formalized in the IPSO framework 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Charilogis2022"
literal "false"

\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\delta(t)=\left|\sum^{m}_{i=1}f_{i}(t)-\sum^{m}_{i=1}f_{i}(t-1)\right|
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\ensuremath{m}$
\end_inset

 denotes the number of particles within the subpopulation,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\ensuremath{f_{i}(t)}$
\end_inset

 represents the objective cost value of the 
\begin_inset Formula $\ensuremath{i}$
\end_inset

-th particle at generation 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 The localized stagnation state indicator 
\begin_inset Formula $\ensuremath{\zeta(t)}$
\end_inset

 is defined as:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\zeta(t)=\begin{cases}
1, & \text{if }\delta(t)=0\\
0, & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Using the real-time feedback from 
\begin_inset Formula $\ensuremath{\zeta(t)}$
\end_inset

,
 the cumulative stagnation tracking matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\ensuremath{S_{\delta}(t)}$
\end_inset

 and its corresponding density ratio 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{\delta}(t)$
\end_inset

 are computed dynamically:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
S_{\delta}(t)=\sum^{t}_{k=1}\zeta(k)
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
C_{\delta}(t)=\frac{S_{\delta}(t)}{t}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

The adaptive inertia weight 
\begin_inset Formula $\ensuremath{\omega(t)}$
\end_inset

 supplied to the velocity kinematic updating core is formally governed by the bound-constrained formulation
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Charilogis2022"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 building upon the dynamic inertia weight control concepts introduced in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Shi1998"
literal "false"

\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\omega(t)=\max\left(\omega_{\min},\min\left(\omega_{\max},\omega_{\max}-C_{\delta}(t)\cdot(\omega_{\max}-\omega_{\min})\right)\right)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega_{\max}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega_{\min}$
\end_inset

 represent the predefined boundary coefficients governing the dynamic momentum spectrum.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection

\series bold
Adaptive Simulated Annealing Acceptance Criterion
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To balance the exploitation of local optima with the necessity of maintaining exploratory pressure,
 a stochastic acceptance mechanism is integrated into the leader update phase of each island.
 Traditional PSO variants typically employ a greedy selection rule,
 which often leads to rapid convergence toward suboptimal regions in non-convex,
 high-dimensional landscapes 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Poli2007,Salgotra2026"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 To mitigate this,
 our approach implements an Adaptive Simulated Annealing Acceptance Criterion,
 allowing the sub-population leader (
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{P}_{\text{best},k}$
\end_inset

) to occasionally accept inferior solutions based on a temperature-dependent probability inspired by the classic Simulated Annealing framework 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Metropolis1953,Kirkpatrick1983,Kuo2022"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta=f(\mathbf{X}_{\text{best},k})-f(\mathbf{P}_{\text{best},k})$
\end_inset

 represent the fitness difference between the current best particle within sub-cluster 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{k}$
\end_inset

 and the recorded sub-cluster leader.
 The probability
\begin_inset Formula $\ensuremath{P_{\text{acc}}}$
\end_inset

of accepting a potentially worse configuration is defined as:
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
P_{\text{acc}}=\begin{cases}
1.0, & \text{if }\Delta<0\\[6pt]
\exp\left(-\frac{\Delta}{T\cdot\bar{\Delta}_{k}+\epsilon}\right), & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 represents the current normalized temperature under a linear cooling schedule,
 calculated as 
\begin_inset Formula $T=\max\left(1.0-\text{progress},10^{-4}\right)$
\end_inset

 to enforce a minimum thermal floor,
 with
\begin_inset Formula $\ensuremath{\text{progress}}$
\end_inset

denoting the relative execution progress (
\begin_inset Formula $t/t_{\max}$
\end_inset

).
 To ensure robustness across diverse objective function scales,
 an adaptive scaling factor 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{\Delta}_{k}$
\end_inset

 is maintained locally for each sub-cluster 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

,
 updating incrementally via an Exponential Moving Average (EMA) as 
\begin_inset Formula $\ensuremath{\bar{\Delta}_{k}\leftarrow0.9\cdot\bar{\Delta}_{k}+0.1\cdot\Delta}$
\end_inset

 for every positive fitness delta observed.
 The term 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon=10^{-9}$
\end_inset

 serves as a numerical stability constant to prevent division by zero.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

This mechanism facilitates a transition from a high-exploration phase at the onset of the optimization process,
 where the acceptance of inferior moves enables the algorithm to escape local attractors,to a high-exploitation phase as 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 approaches zero,
 effectively narrowing the search towards the global optimum 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Kirkpatrick1983,Kuo2022"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 By decoupling the leader update from strict greedy selection,
 the algorithm exhibits enhanced resilience against the "saddle point" problem in high-dimensional spaces 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Dauphin2014"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 ensuring a more thorough traversal of the search space before settling into a terminal configuration 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Fernandez2022,Gillner2026"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 This adaptive control,
 combined with the parallel island-model paradigm,
 provides a robust defense against premature stagnation without the prohibitive computational overhead associated with global,
 non-localized simulated annealing schedules 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Charilogis2022,Charilogis2023,daSilveira2020,Abadlia2017"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection

\series bold
Hybrid Local Search Phase
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To balance global stochastic exploration with high-precision exploitation,
 Parallel OPSO incorporates a memetic hybrid local search phase 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Petalas2007"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 based on the deterministic Broyden–Fletcher–Goldfarb–Shanno (BFGS) quasi-Newton algorithm 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Nocedal2006"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 During execution,
 the local search procedure is stochastically triggered on the global best position
\begin_inset Formula $\ensuremath{\mathbf{g}_{\text{best}}}$
\end_inset

with a predefined probability 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{ls}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\texttt{(\texttt{local\_search\_rate}).}$
\end_inset

Additionally,
 a final deterministic refinement step is mandatorily executed inside the 
\begin_inset Formula $\texttt{done()}$
\end_inset

routine upon meeting the swarm stopping criteria.
 The BFGS algorithm iteratively refines the target position vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\ensuremath{\mathbf{x}_{k}}$
\end_inset

 along a search direction 
\begin_inset Formula $\ensuremath{\mathbf{p}_{k}=-H_{k}\nabla f(\mathbf{x}_{k})}$
\end_inset

,
 where 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{k}$
\end_inset

 represents an approximate inverse Hessian matrix.
 At each local step 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{k}$
\end_inset

 is dynamically updated using the spatial difference vector
\begin_inset Formula $\ensuremath{\mathbf{s}_{k}}=\ensuremath{\mathbf{x}_{k+1}}-\ensuremath{\mathbf{x}_{k}}$
\end_inset

 and the gradient difference vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\ensuremath{\mathbf{y}_{k}=\nabla f(\mathbf{x}_{k+1})-\nabla f(\mathbf{x}_{k})}$
\end_inset

 according to:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
H_{k+1}=(I-\rho_{k}\mathbf{s}_{k}\mathbf{y}^{T}_{k})H_{k}(I-\rho_{k}\mathbf{y}_{k}\mathbf{s}^{T}_{k})+\rho_{k}\mathbf{s}_{k}\mathbf{s}^{T}_{k}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\ensuremath{\rho_{k}=(\mathbf{y}^{T}_{k}\mathbf{s}_{k})^{-1}}$
\end_inset

.
 This gradient-based coupling guarantees superlinear local convergence characteristics,
 refining the stochastic candidate solutions of OPSO to exact numerical optima.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection

\series bold
Subpopulation Termination Criterion via Similarity Check
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:similarity-check"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In parallel multi-swarm architectures,
 determining the optimal execution boundary for each autonomous subpopulation is critical.
 Halting an island prematurely deprives the global swarm of valuable population diversity and high-quality coordinates during migration phases 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "daSilveira2020,Abadlia2017"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Conversely,
 allowing a stagnated subpopulation to execute until the global maximum iteration limit leads to wasted processor cycles and unnecessary energy consumption 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Charilogis2023,Kyrou2026"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 To resolve this trade-off,
 each individual island 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 dynamically monitors its own search trajectory via a localized termination state 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{(\text{TC}_{k})}$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

To ensure sufficient initial exploration and avoid false-positive termination signals caused by early stochastic fluctuations,
 a mandatory warm-up period is enforced.
 Localized convergence diagnostics remain inactive until the generation counter satisfies:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
t\ge t_{\min},\quad\text{where }t_{\min}=50
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Upon crossing this threshold,
 the algorithm tracks the fitness progression of island 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 at every generation 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 by calculating the absolute difference between its current and previous local best objective values:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\Delta f_{k}(t)=\left|f(\mathbf{L}_{\text{best},k}(t))-f(\mathbf{L}_{\text{best},k}(t-1))\right|
\end{equation}

\end_inset

To evaluate whether 
\begin_inset Formula $\ensuremath{\Delta f_{k}(t)}$
\end_inset

 reflects genuine mathematical convergence rather than a transient plateau,
 the framework embeds the Similarity Check heuristic proposed by Tsoulos et al.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Tsoulos2021"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Conventional termination metrics often rely on instantaneous point-to-point differences,
 making them highly sensitive to noise in complex fitness landscapes.
 In contrast,
 the Similarity Check evaluates the convergence behavior over a sliding historical window of 
\begin_inset Formula $\ensuremath{W=25}$
\end_inset

 consecutive iterations.
 Specifically,
 island 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 is declared locally converged (
\begin_inset Formula $TC_{k}=\text{true}$
\end_inset

) when the average objective improvement falls below a predefined tolerance threshold 
\begin_inset Formula $\varepsilon$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{1}{W}\sum^{W-1}_{m=0}\Delta f_{k}(t-m)<\epsilon
\end{equation}

\end_inset

From an implementation perspective,
 each parallel worker thread maintains an isolated heuristic evaluator instance (
\begin_inset Formula $\texttt{threadSimilarity[\ensuremath{k}]}$
\end_inset

).
 This design eliminates thread contention and lock overheads,
 enabling fully decoupled convergence checks across processing cores 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "OpenMP2024,Charilogis2023"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 The aggregate count of stagnated islands is continuously tracked as:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
S_{\text{term}}=\sum^{K}_{k=1}\mathbb{I}(\text{TC}_{k}=\text{true})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{I}(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 denotes the indicator function.
 When 
\begin_inset Formula $\ensuremath{S_{\text{term}}\ge Q},$
\end_inset

the global termination strategy 
\begin_inset Formula $\ensuremath{(\text{TS})}$
\end_inset

 is triggered 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Kyrou2026,Tsoulos2021"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Finally,
 the maximum iteration count 
\begin_inset Formula $\ensuremath{t_{\max}}$
\end_inset

 serves as an absolute safety ceiling (
\begin_inset Formula $\ensuremath{t\ge t_{\max}}$
\end_inset

),
 guaranteeing bounded computational time under all execution conditions.
\end_layout

