\begin_layout Subsection

\series bold
Computational Complexity Analysis
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The computational complexity of the proposed algorithm is analyzed per iteration,
 considering the number of particles 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

,
 the dimensionality of the search space 
\begin_inset Formula $D$
\end_inset

,
 and the number of subpopulations (islands) 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $n=N/K$
\end_inset

 be the number of particles per island.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

The total complexity per iteration is dominated by the evaluation of the objective function,
 the velocity/position updates,
 the hybrid local search procedure,
 and the communication overhead.
 The breakdown is as follows:
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Objective Function Evaluation:
 The primary cost is associated with the evaluation of the objective function.
 In each iteration,
 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 evaluations are performed.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{cost}$
\end_inset

 denotes the computational cost of the objective function 
\begin_inset Formula $f(\mathbf{x})$
\end_inset

,
 the complexity is 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{O}(N\cdot f_{cost})$
\end_inset

.
 Due to the OpenMP parallelization,
 this is distributed across 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

 threads,
 reducing the execution time to 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{O}(\frac{N}{K}\cdot f_{cost})$
\end_inset

 per thread,
 assuming uniform load balancing 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Charilogis2023,OpenMP2024"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Particle Update Mechanism:
 The velocity and position update operations for all 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 particles across 
\begin_inset Formula $D$
\end_inset

 dimensions require 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{O}(N\cdot D)$
\end_inset

 time.
 Similar to the objective function,
 this is parallelized as 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{O}(\frac{N}{K}\cdot D)$
\end_inset

 per subpopulation 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Charilogis2023,Shi1998"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Hybrid Local Search Phase:
 The deterministic BFGS quasi-Newton refinement is stochastically invoked on the global best solution with probability 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{ls}$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{\text{evals}}$
\end_inset

 denote the average number of objective function evaluations required by the BFGS gradient calculations and line search per local execution.
 The computational cost of matrix updates for a 
\begin_inset Formula $D$
\end_inset

-dimensional search space is 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{O}(D^{2})$
\end_inset

.
 Thus,
 the amortized cost per iteration for the hybrid refinement phase is 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{O}\left(p_{ls}\cdot(L_{\text{evals}}\cdot f_{\text{cost}}+D^{2})\right)$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Petalas2007,Nocedal2006"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Inter-Island Propagation:
 The migration mechanism involves sorting particles by fitness within subpopulations and transferring them.
 Sorting 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 particles takes 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{O}(n\log n)$
\end_inset

.
 Given that the migration occurs every 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

 iterations (propagation rate),
 the amortized complexity per iteration for the migration phase is 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{O}(\frac{K\cdot n\log n}{P})$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Charilogis2023,daSilveira2020,Abadlia2017"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

Combining the above,
 the total computational complexity per iteration 
\begin_inset Formula $\ensuremath{T_{t}}$
\end_inset

 is expressed as:
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
T_{t}\approx\mathcal{O}\left(\frac{N}{K}(f_{\text{cost}}+D)+p_{ls}\cdot\left(L_{\text{evals}}\cdot f_{\text{cost}}+D^{2}\right)+\frac{N\log\left(\frac{N}{K}\right)}{P}\right)
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

As 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

 is typically significantly larger than 1 (e.g.,
 
\begin_inset Formula $P=10$
\end_inset

) and 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{ls}$
\end_inset

 is set to a moderate rate (e.g.,
 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{ls}\le0.1$
\end_inset

),
 the propagation and local search overheads remain negligible compared to the objective function evaluations.
 Consequently,
 the algorithm exhibits linear scaling with respect to the number of particles and dimensions,
 while the parallel efficiency is optimized by the factor 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

,
 allowing the algorithm to maintain high performance in high-dimensional optimization problems 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Sun2022,Charilogis2023,Kyrou2026"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

